---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const tenure = assertion('lima-rents-and-the-townships-own')

const outside = assertion('three-thousand-people-live-outside-households')

const fence = assertion('the-census-drew-the-block-on-the-fence')

// `categories` and `values` are what the bar renderer reads. This helper was written with a
// line's `x` and `series` for a phase, so all five of this page's charts drew a trace over two
// undefined arrays and rendered empty — silently, because the table under each one was right
// and nothing typechecked the spec. See the check added to the gate.
const bars = (a: typeof tenure, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle },
  )

const outsideSpec = bars(outside, 'People')
---

<Article slug="who-is-not-in-a-house-at-all">
  <AssertionCard assertion={outside} />
  <p>
    Everything else on this page counts housing units and the people in them. This is the
    remainder: 3,522 people the 2020 census found in Allen County who were not living in a
    household, in forty-three of its 3,552 blocks. The county records no juvenile facility, no
    psychiatric-hospital population and no military quarters.
  </p>
  <ChartFigure
    id="group-quarters"
    title="Allen County's group quarters population by type, 2020"
    caption="2020 Census Redistricting Data Summary File, Table P5, summed from block records. 'Shelters and group homes' is the census's 'other noninstitutional facilities', which its Appendix B defines as emergency and transitional shelters for people experiencing homelessness, group homes intended for adults, and residential treatment centres."
    columns={['Type', 'People']}
    rows={outside.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="group-quarters" set:html={JSON.stringify(outsideSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Lima holds 2,210 of the 3,522 on a third of the county's people, including every one of the
    1,513 in correctional facilities. The nursing homes are the only institution the county
    spreads out: 966 people in fifteen blocks across the city and seven townships, the largest of
    them holding 132. The 788 in student housing are mostly one campus — 494 of them at Bluffton
    University — and the 255 in shelters, group homes and treatment centres are mostly one
    downtown tract, which holds 124 of them.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fence} />
  <p>
    For two phases this site could say that one Lima block held 1,360 people and no housing units
    and could not say what it was. The county's address file put a single point inside it, an
    auto wrecking yard, and put the three institutional addresses it carries half a mile west.
    The join that worked was between two files the Census Bureau draws from the same polygon: the
    block and the landmark carry the same land area to the square metre and the same internal
    point to seven decimal places, for Allen Correctional Institution and again for Oakwood. One
    of the three names in the county's file is a prison that closed in 2004.
  </p>
  <p>
    That also explains a puzzle on the elections side of this corpus. The precinct containing
    both institutions has the lowest voter-registration ratio of the county's eighty-eight, at
    0.28 per resident against a median of 0.68. Take the 1,513 out and it is 0.53, inside the
    band every other precinct occupies. The lowest registration rate in Allen County is a
    prison, and nothing on the face of the map says so.
  </p>
</Article>
